	Documentation Changes, Revision 1			     2-Sep-93


	Page 1-11.  Add the following section:

	|	Implementation of System Calls
	|
	|	Assembly language and high-level language programs use
	|	system calls to transfer execution from a user program
	|	running in rings 4 through 7 to system code located in
	|	rings 0 through 3.  If you need to know the details of
	|	how this transfer normally occurs, consider the
	|	example of the ?GNAME system call.
	|
	|	For 32 bit programs, the symbol ?GNAME is defined as a
	|	MASM macro in SYSID.32.SR so that it expands to the
	|	following code where 111 indicates this is the
	|	?GNAME system call.
	|
	|				XJSR @6
	|				111
	|	
	|	Location 6 contains the 32 bit address SYST which is
	|	defined in the URT32.LB library.  The SYST routine
	|	contains the following code.
	|
	|			SYST:	WPSH	3,3
	|				LCALL	06000000000,0,0
	|				WBR	SYS10
	|				ISZTS
	|			SYS10:	ISZTS
	|				LDAFP	3
	|				WPOPJ
	|
	|	For 16 bit programs, the symbol ?GNAME is defined as a
	|	MASM macro in SYSID.16.SR so that it expands to the
	|	following code where 111 indicates this is the
	|	?GNAME system call.
	|
	|				JSR	@17
	|				111
	|
	|	Location 17 contains the 16 bit address SYST which is
	|	defined in the URT16.LB library.  The SYST routine
	|	contains the following code.
	|
	|			SYST:	PSH	3,3
	|				LCALL	06000000000,0,0
	|				JMP	SYS11
	|				LDA	3,40
	|				ISZ	0,3
	|			SYS10:	ISZ	0,3
	|				LDA	3,41
	|				POPJ
	|			SYS11:	LDA	3,40
	|				JMP	SYS10


